decimal code to prevent browser errors.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Why is URL encoding necessary?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"URL encoding is necessary because characters like spaces, question marks, ampersands, and slashes have special meanings in URL structures. If you include these characters raw in query parameters, browsers will misinterpret them, causing broken links, database errors, or security vulnerabilities during data transmission.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"When should I decode a URL?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"You should decode a URL when you retrieve raw tracking links or query parameters that contain encoded percent symbols (like %20 for spaces or %26 for ampersands). Decoding translates these hexadecimal strings back into human-readable text, making it easy to read tracking data or analyze website analytics parameters.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Can encoded URLs improve compatibility?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Yes, encoding URLs guarantees compatibility across all browsers, web servers, database systems, and API endpoints. It ensures that complex strings containing non-English characters or mathematical symbols are transmitted securely without getting corrupted or rejected by network routers and content management platforms.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Is URL encoding safe?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Yes, URL encoding is completely safe. It is a standard web protocol that does not modify the underlying data; it simply formats it for secure transmission. Additionally, our tool processes all encoding and decoding locally in your browser, keeping your text inputs and URLs completely private.\"}}]}13:[[\"$\",\"script\",null,{\"type\":\"application/ld+json\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"{\\\"@context\\\":\\\"https://schema.org\\\",\\\"@type\\\":\\\"WebApplication\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"URL E